DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ggnnoopp utility is used for setting up transparent providers on existing
     ones.  Its main purpose is testing other GEOM classes, as it allows
     forced provider removal and I/O error simulation with a given probabil‐
     ity.  It also gathers statistics on the number of read, write, delete,
     getattr, flush, and other requests, and the number of bytes read and
     written.  ggnnoopp can also be used as a good starting point for implementing
     new GEOM classes.

     The first argument to ggnnoopp indicates an action to be performed:

     ccrreeaattee     Set up a transparent provider on the given devices.  If the
                operation succeeds, the new provider should appear with name
                _/_d_e_v_/⟨_d_e_v⟩_._n_o_p.  The kernel module _g_e_o_m___n_o_p_._k_o will be loaded
                if it is not loaded already.

     ccoonnffiigguurree  Configure existing transparent provider.  At the moment it is
                only used for changing failure probability.

     ddeessttrrooyy    Turn off the given transparent providers.

     rreesseett      Reset statistics for the given transparent providers.

     lliisstt       See geom(8).

     ssttaattuuss     See geom(8).

     llooaadd       See geom(8).

     uunnllooaadd     See geom(8).

     Additional options:

     --cc _c_o_u_n_t___u_n_t_i_l___f_a_i_l  Specifies the number of I/O requests to allow before
                          setting the read, write and delay failure probabili‐
                          ties.

     --dd _d_e_l_a_y_m_s_e_c         Specifies the delay of the requests in milliseconds.
                          Note that requests will be delayed before they are
                          sent to the backing device.

     --ee _e_r_r_o_r             Specifies the error number to return on failure.

     --ff                   Force the removal of the specified provider.

     --oo _o_f_f_s_e_t            Where to begin on the original provider.

     --pp _s_t_r_i_p_e_s_i_z_e        Value of the stripesize property of the transparent
                          provider.

     --PP _s_t_r_i_p_e_o_f_f_s_e_t      Value of the stripeoffset property of the transpar‐
                          ent provider.

     --qq _r_d_e_l_a_y_p_r_o_b        Specifies read delay probability in percent.

     --rr _r_f_a_i_l_p_r_o_b         Specifies read failure probability in percent.

     --ss _s_i_z_e              Size of the transparent provider.

     --SS _s_e_c_s_i_z_e           Sector size of the transparent provider.

     --ww _w_f_a_i_l_p_r_o_b         Specifies write failure probability in percent.

     --vv                   Be more verbose.

     --xx _w_d_e_l_a_y_p_r_o_b        Specifies write delay probability in percent.

     --zz _p_h_y_s_p_a_t_h          Physical path of the transparent provider.

     --ZZ _g_n_o_p_n_a_m_e          The name of the new provider.  The suffix “.nop”
                          will be appended to the provider name.

SSYYSSCCTTLL VVAARRIIAABBLLEESS
     The following sysctl(8) variables can be used to control the behavior of
     the NNOOPP GEOM class.  The default value is shown next to each variable.

     _k_e_r_n_._g_e_o_m_._n_o_p_._d_e_b_u_g: 0
             Debug level of the NNOOPP GEOM class.  This can be set to a number
             between 0 and 2 inclusive.  If set to 0, minimal debug informa‐
             tion is printed.  If set to 1, basic debug information is logged
             along with the I/O requests that were returned as errors.  If set
             to 2, the maximum amount of debug information is printed includ‐
             ing all I/O requests.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     Exit status is 0 on success, and 1 if the command fails.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The following example shows how to create a transparent provider for disk
     _/_d_e_v_/_d_a_0 with 50% write failure probability, and how to destroy it.

           gnop create -v -w 50 da0
           gnop destroy -v da0.nop

     The traffic statistics for the given transparent providers can be ob‐
     tained with the lliisstt command.  The example below shows the number of
     bytes written with newfs(8):

           gnop create da0
           newfs /dev/da0.nop
           gnop list

